    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The other day, I was waiting on a train platform—angrily. The train was late, which meant I would be late to meet a friend at the theatre. It didn’t help that I had never been to the theatre before. I had no idea how long it would take to walk from the station. “Running just a bit late,” I texted with regret."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Like many people, I hate being late. However, I often find myself running late. What’s the problem?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Michaela Thomas, a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "psychologist",
            "zh": "心理学家"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", says I’m a “time optimist”. “Time optimists” often "
          },
          {
            "t": "underline",
            "v": "underestimate"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " how long something will take. As a result, they are often late for meetings or rush things at the last minute."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Time optimists” regularly get into trouble. They make those around them angry, and they suffer a lot of stress themselves. How can they fix their ways?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Grace Pacie has written a book about “time optimists”. She believes there is a "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "subconscious",
            "zh": "潜意识的"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " behaviour behind our lateness—the fear of being early. “We usually plan to be on time, not ahead of time,” she tells me. “It took me a long while to understand that if I’m going on a journey that’s going to take twenty minutes, I shouldn’t leave the house with just twenty minutes to "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "spare",
            "ref": "v-spare"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". It’s important to leave time for things to go wrong. Not all the lights are going to be green.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Pacie suggests that “time optimists” set a pre-event deadline. If your fitness class starts at 8 p.m., for example, tell yourself to arrive at 7:30 p.m. It may be a good idea to find something to do near the location beforehand."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Many “time optimists” (and those around them) think they need to improve their time management skills. But Fuschia Sirois, a psychology professor, says that’s not always true. Lateness is often about poor mood management. Sometimes, people have too much going on at work and with family, and they often go for the easy fix—"
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "procrastinating",
            "zh": "拖延"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p8",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "One solution, Sirois suggests, is to have a more "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "realistic",
            "ref": "v-realistic"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " relationship with your future self. For example, you are up against a deadline but feel tired and want to put off the task. Ask yourself, “Will I magically feel energetic on the second day? If I can’t really do it all tomorrow, why not start doing it today?” In fact, in the future, you will probably feel even more stressed about the task when it’s late."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
